[ ] Project workspaces to the application graph in Core =v5-projection $The core code change of Decision 43. Replace the v4 projection in clearhead-core rdf/project.rs and rdf/mod.rs with the mapping the spec defines, emitting no v4 terms. Prove it against the spec's fixture and shapes at Core's spec-conformance boundary, as the JSON schema conformance already runs, and check reasoner consistency with the ontology. Expect the graph to grow about tenfold per action; measure load and query time on this workspace before and after. ADDED 2026-10-01: the spec now requires times as written (no offset unless the file wrote one; date-only as xsd:date). Core's domain stores DateTime<Local>, so today's projection stamps the machine's offset and differs between time zones; the projection, or the domain, must keep what was written. CORRECTION 2026-10-02: the graph grows about 2x, not tenfold. Measured on the spec fixture: v4 emits 109 triples, 23 of them ws: storage facts that Decision 44 removes (86 left); the V5 expected graph has 172. Still measure load and query time, but expect no step change. RETARGETED 2026-10-02 by Decision 45: Core emits the application vocabulary, not CCO; most of today's v4 projection survives with renamed terms and the fixture's expected-app.ttl as its oracle. Keep times as written.$ !1 ^2026-10-01T20:22 <01a0fb89-fdcd-722f-a0ab-5399b7a3c0cb #01a0faa2-7184-74d1-ae6c-1959f4af7fe3
[ ] Move the saved queries to the application vocabulary =v5-queries $Rewrite every saved query in clearhead-cli src/queries and the CLI's sparql modules against the new graph, plus this workspace's .clearhead/queries/for-human.sparql. Row shapes stay as they are, so nvim and other clients do not change; the existing query tests are the net, so freeze their expected rows before rewriting. The competency queries in ontology v5/queries are the working templates. ADDED 2026-10-01: the graph will carry no storage facts (spec convention 3, the human's call: no file paths, lines, roots or calendar ids, since another backend such as sqlite would have none). Today's rows include source_file and source_line, used by nvim to jump to source, and some index queries join through the ws:Workspace node. Keep the rows unchanged by having the implementation add locations after the query, joining each row's IRI against the loaded workspace; remove every ws: pattern from the queries. REVISED 2026-10-01 with the human: rows lose source_file, source_line and charter_root instead of the CLI adding them back; clients get locations from the new locate command. The row shapes therefore do change for those columns, and nvim moves with them (nvim-locate). RETARGETED 2026-10-02 by Decision 45: queries use the application vocabulary (the spike's view queries are the template, about a third of v4's length); file and line stay in the graph, so rows keep their location columns and nvim is unchanged. Templates: docs/history/query-surface-spike/view/{for-human,agenda,unscheduled}.rq, which already return v4's answers on the spike workspace.$ !2 ^2026-10-01T20:22 <01a0fb02-e644-7317-88ad-1e76ecfb2b0e #01a0faa2-71a3-70a1-91c5-a485dedd4de2
[ ] Retire the v4 vocabulary everywhere =retire-v4 $Remove the v4 namespace constants and fixtures from clearhead-core, the ontology's v4 directory, examples and queries, its pytest suite and pyproject deps, and V4_DESIGN.md; point specifications/ontology.md readers at the ontology's docs/domain.md. Decide then whether clearhead.us keeps serving the v4 vocabulary for anyone holding its IRIs (cool URIs do not change) or drops it with the site.$ !3 ^2026-10-01T20:22 <01a0faa2-71a3-70a1-91c5-a485dedd4de2 #01a0faa2-71c0-7365-9b59-8baa79bf9766
[ ] Extend the graph fixture to recurring plans and objective states =v5-fixture-gaps $The graph fixture in specifications/examples/conformance/graph covers every action field and state, charters, objectives, contexts and waits, but not: an .ics recurring plan with a materialized occurrence (when/recurrence, RRULE text, occurrence as part of the recurring action), and an objective state, whose vocabulary objectives.md does not define yet (Decision 5 says objectives have state, confirmed by an agent). Settle the objective state values with the objectives-into-Core work, then add both to the workspace, expected.ttl and the shapes, with an invalid graph for each new rule. Decision 45: the gaps apply to both expected-app.ttl and expected.ttl.$ !3 ^2026-10-01T22:27 #01a0fb14-c5e6-73d8-9c0d-f56f11ce00a6
[ ] Show the actions that serve an objective =objective-actions-view $Moved out of the objectives action, 2026-10-01: answering it is a query (an objective's plans, then everything they contain), which V5 makes a has-continuant-part walk from the plans that name the objective. Write it as a saved query once v5-queries has moved the queries to the new graph, then surface it in the CLI and nvim like the other named queries.$ !3 ^2026-10-01T22:35 <01a0faa2-71a3-70a1-91c5-a485dedd4de2 #01a0fb1c-4455-7218-bc00-d4d38247de78
[ ] Distinguish scheduled from due in the mapping =scheduled-vs-due $Found by the query-surface spike, 2026-10-02: a scheduled time (not before) and a due time (by) both project as a condition whose description carries a datetime on its bearer, so the graph cannot tell a start from a deadline; only the UUIDv5 role, which is a hash, differs. Neither the shapes nor the reasoner caught it, since both are valid and consistent. Find standard terms that say which bound a time is (for example a description of a temporal interval that the time starts or finishes, CCO interval starts / interval finishes, or OWL-Time), update ontology.md, the fixture, the shapes, and add an invalid graph or a query to the gate that must recover the difference. Also decide how a date and a datetime compare, since times are now as written. PLAN 2026-10-02 (for the next agent; ask before deviating from the recommendation). RECOMMENDED: one window condition per action, as the DSL spec already words it (action_file_format.md: '@' and ':' are the lower and upper bounds of one inclusive window). The Performance Specification's description describes a BFO temporal interval (obo:BFO_0000202), the window; '@' is its first instant (obo:BFO_0000222 has first instant) and ':' its last (obo:BFO_0000224 has last instant), each a BFO temporal instant (obo:BFO_0000203) designated (cco:ont00001916) by a CCO Temporal Instant Identifier (cco:ont00000540) whose bearer has the datetime (cco:ont00001767) or date (cco:ont00001771) as written. All BFO/CCO, no new vocabulary; about 20 triples for a two-bound window, acceptable now that Decision 45 makes CCO the meaning layer, not the queried one. ALTERNATIVE: W3C OWL-Time (time:Interval, time:hasBeginning, time:hasEnd, time:inXSDDateTimeStamp): lighter, but a second upper vocabulary beside BFO; reject unless the BFO form fails HermiT. STEPS: 1) ontology: add a window example (an action with both bounds, one with only a due date) to v5/examples, extend cq3 or add cq9 'what is due by a date' that must tell start from deadline, add its expected CSV, make -C ontology/v5 test green. 2) spec ontology.md: replace the scheduledDateTime/dueDateTime rows and the when/scheduled, when/due roles with when/window, when/window/start, when/window/end (and their identifier and bearer roles). 3) fixture: regenerate expected.ttl with docs/spikes/2026-10-02-query-surface/gen_expected.py (update its condition helper), then extend graph.shapes.ttl (a window has at most one first and one last instant; a time-bearing description must describe a window) and add invalid graphs for each new rule. 4) scripts/check-graph-shapes.py and validate-pinned green, then commit spec + ontology + platform. OPEN, settle with app-vocabulary: how a date and a datetime compare once times are as written (the spike compared date prefixes as strings); the application vocabulary may expose a normalized comparable value beside the written one. RESCOPED 2026-10-02 with the human: this action is now step 1 only (prove the window pattern in ontology/v5 examples and a competency query that must tell start from deadline, HermiT green), which settles the meaning before app-vocabulary. Steps 2 and 3 move into app-to-cco: the mapping CONSTRUCT emits the window and generates expected.ttl, so nothing here rewrites ontology.md's role table or regenerates expected.ttl with gen_expected.py, both of which the later actions replace. WIDENED 2026-10-02 with the human: step 1 is now a small time-model spike, since time, date, duration and recurrence are one small, critical part of the architecture. Duration is settled (Decision 46). Still open, to settle by the spike, not by argument: a date-only bound is a whole day (from its start for @, to its end for :); floating versus offset times compared at query time in the viewer's zone, not baked into the graph; the window pattern in CCO. Method: about ten awkward lines (date-only due, floating beside offset, D with and without @, a VEVENT occurrence, a recurring VTODO), three real queries (today's agenda, overdue, what fits in 30 minutes now), two or three candidate app: shapes; record the one that answers them plainly and correctly, with the CCO window as its meaning. SETTLED 2026-10-02 (Decision 47, human): a date-only bound is the whole day, for @ and : alike, as calendars treat all-day events. The spike now tests that rule and the open zone question (floating versus offset, compared in the viewer's zone at query time), and writes both into action_file_format.md's bound semantics.$ !1 ^2026-10-02T00:28 #01a0fb83-8b3a-700c-8776-4c5749b3e82d
[ ] Define the application vocabulary in the spec =app-vocabulary $Per Decision 45: a fresh-named ClearHead application vocabulary, owned by the specification beside the JSON schemas. Start from the spike's view and v4, but complete: every DSL, charter and objective field and state, plus file and line (optional for another backend), so the CCO graph can be generated from it. Rewrite specifications/ontology.md around it (the application graph first; CCO as its meaning, mapped in the ontology repo), add its SHACL shapes, and add expected-app.ttl to the graph fixture: the workspace's exact application graph. The name waits on the human. Starting material: docs/history/query-surface-spike/view/view.rq (the sketch, a subset: add descriptions, created and completed times, objectives, metrics, aliases, charter parents, plans, file and line). NAMED 2026-10-02: prefix app:, namespace https://clearhead.us/vocab/app/v1#, absorbing ws:.$ !1 ^2026-10-02T00:35 <01a0fb83-8b3a-700c-8776-4c5749b3e82d #01a0fb89-fdba-7302-9609-92dfed6469d5
[ ] Map the application vocabulary to CCO in the ontology =app-to-cco $Per Decision 45 the ontology becomes the mapping: one SPARQL CONSTRUCT from the application vocabulary to CCO v2.2 and IAO, in the ontology repo, defining what each application term means. Helper-node IRIs must be computable inside SPARQL (no UUIDv5 there), for example a hash of owner and role; update the spec's Helper node IRIs and expected.ttl to match. Gate: the mapping of the fixture's expected-app.ttl is isomorphic to expected.ttl, which passes the CCO shapes, HermiT and the ontology's verify rules; wire it into validate-pinned. Consider rewriting the ontology's examples in the application vocabulary and mapping them for reasoning. ADDED 2026-10-02 (the human's condition on the name: a clean path from app to the domain ontology): the gate also fails if any term in the application vocabulary is not used by the mapping, so no app term exists without a meaning. Includes scheduled-vs-due steps 2 and 3: the window (first and last instant) is emitted by the mapping, the spec's helper-node roles and the shapes follow it, with an invalid graph per new rule.$ !1 ^2026-10-02T00:35 <01a0fb89-fdba-7302-9609-92dfed6469d5 #01a0fb89-fdcd-722f-a0ab-5399b7a3c0cb
[ ] Make duration an action field of its own =standalone-duration $Decision 46. Spec: list D in action_file_format.md's field order, say minutes (drop the ISO 8601 durations sentence), retire E001 in linting.md, and in ics_schedule_spec.md map @ plus D to DTSTART plus DURATION (nothing placed without @). Grammar: D becomes its own _metadata choice, so @… D60 still parses; update highlights.scm and the corpus. Schema: durationMinutes describes the expected length of the work. Core: two bugs found 2026-10-02 fix themselves here: the parser reads D only under @ (D after : is silently dropped), and the formatter writes D only when @ is present (a D without @ would be lost on format). Retire check_duration_without_do_date. Calendar: VEVENT sync emits DURATION from D when @ is present (today it copies DTEND verbatim and never maps D). SIGIL OPEN 2026-10-02: the human pointed out D cannot stand alone (it would be excluded from titles); this action waits on 'Pick the duration sigil'. Today's corpus already shows the bug: lint fixtures 'Meeting D60' and 'Task with duration D90' parse D60 and D90 as part of the title. SIGIL SETTLED 2026-10-02: | (for example |60). Add | to METADATA_CHARS; keep reading the old @… D60 form for now so existing files still parse, and have the formatter write |60 (migration by format). Decision 47 edge: a date-only @ syncs as an all-day event; its duration stays on the action (RFC 5545 sizes a DATE start only in whole days).$ !1 ^2026-10-02T12:10 <01a0fe08-fd5e-7315-9019-8974bb322e24 #01a0fe06-39ad-7762-a527-3164ef66920a
